Packetisation of Chrome's opening flight, TCP and QUIC.

  tool     cmd/arrival
  client   Google Chrome 152.0.7977.66, macOS 25.5.0 arm64, headless=new,
           fresh --user-data-dir per launch
  target   127.0.0.1 (an IP literal, so no DNS resolution of any kind occurs
           and no HTTPS RR can be fetched)
  method   neither listener ever answers; the client sends its opening flight
           on connect and then retransmits

TCP -- how many writes carry the ClientHello
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tcp 1: ClientHello 1919 B in 1 write(s): [1919B@0.63ms]
tcp 2: ClientHello 1951 B in 1 write(s): [1951B@0.10ms]
tcp 4: ClientHello 2015 B in 1 write(s): [2015B@0.09ms]

Every hello arrived in a single read. Loopback MTU is 16384, so this measures
WRITE boundaries, not segments: Chrome hands the whole ClientHello to the
socket in one write and lets the kernel segment it. twiddle's single
raw.Write(wire) in handshake.go is therefore the faithful behaviour, and
deliberately splitting the write would NOT be.

Path segmentation follows from the length alone. At 1919-2015 B a hello spans
two segments at any common MSS (1460, 1440, 1400, 1360) and three only below
MSS ~1008 -- so on TCP the "three packets" case does not arise in practice.

QUIC -- how many datagrams carry the ClientHello
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
udp  1: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @    0.00ms   <-- opening flight
udp  2: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @    2.08ms   <-- opening flight
udp  3: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @  302.63ms   retransmit
udp  4: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @  906.06ms   retransmit
udp  5: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @ 2111.90ms   retransmit
udp  6: 1250 B  Initial  from :55283 @ 4002.58ms   retransmit
udp  7: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 5027.89ms   second attempt, new port
udp  8: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 5028.04ms
udp  9: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 5332.32ms
udp 10: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 5935.15ms
udp 11: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 7139.25ms
udp 12: 1250 B  Initial  from :58801 @ 8949.08ms

The opening flight is TWO Initial datagrams sent back to back, each padded to
Chrome's 1250-byte max packet size. A ~1950-byte ClientHello does not fit one
Initial once the post-quantum key_share is in it, so it is split across CRYPTO
frames in consecutive packets -- which is also what Chaos Protection does on
purpose, splitting and reordering CRYPTO frames and interleaving PADDING/PING.
A larger hello (a real SNI instead of an IP literal, the top ECH bucket, more
ALPN) pushes this to three datagrams; two is the floor, not the norm.

Retransmits back off ~300ms / 600ms / 1200ms / 1900ms, then the attempt is
abandoned and a fresh connection opens from a new source port.

Hello contents, same captures
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1919 B  ECH ext=186  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 1919 B  ECH ext=186  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 1919 B  ECH ext=186  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 1951 B  ECH ext=218  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 1951 B  ECH ext=218  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 1983 B  ECH ext=250  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no
 2015 B  ECH ext=282  exts=18  0xca34=yes  0x12e0=no

Six findings:

1. ECH GREASE is present on 7 of 7 hellos to a bare IP literal. No DNS query
   was made, so no HTTPS RR and no ECHConfig could have been fetched, and no
   DoH resolver was reachable. Chrome sends GREASE ECH unconditionally -- it is
   not gated on secure DNS. See docs/ech.md.

2. The ECH extension length takes exactly the four values 186/218/250/282,
   unchanged from the buckets measured on the older Chrome that produced
   pool/chrome.hex. echGREASELengths in twiddle.go is still correct.

3. 0xca34 is present on every hello and appears in no pooled hello; 0x12e0
   (BoringSSL server_padding) is present on four pooled hellos and on none of
   these. The pooled extension set is not Chrome 152's.

4. All 7 hellos, across two separate Chrome launches with different fresh
   profiles, fingerprint as ONE build (5cf4f560d630d302) once the GREASE cipher
   suite, the GREASE extension values, the extension order and the ECH bucket
   are normalised away. That is the invariant Coherent relies on.

5. 0xca34's BODY varies per connection at a constant 206 bytes. Normalising
   away everything the emitter regenerates still leaves these 7 hellos in two
   distinct canonical forms, and 0xca34 is the field that separates them:

     hello 1: 00cc 04d679090e 08839a648c9b2d0107 08839a648c9b2d0109 ...
     hello 0: 00cc 0582df1302 0108839a648c9b2d0107 08839a648c9b2d010c ...

   The 00cc prefix is a 204-byte list length, and the entries look like
   length-prefixed opaque identifiers -- consistent with Trust Anchor
   Identifiers. Nothing in twiddle regenerates this, so same-build hellos are
   NOT interchangeable and a device pool holding several is sampling real
   variation. See the known-gaps list in README.md.

6. GREASE, measured across all 15 hellos (8 pooled + 7 from Chrome 152), in six
   slots. Two constraints held on every one:

     - the first and last extension draws always DIFFER (BoringSSL's own rule)
     - supported_groups' draw is always the SAME value as the key_share group,
       which the protocol requires: a key_share names an offered group

   Collisions across other slots do occur -- pool[3] drew 0xfafa for both its
   cipher suite and its trailing extension -- so the draws are independent.
   The GREASE key_share entry's payload was 0x00 on all 15.

Reproduce:

  go run ./cmd/arrival -conns 4 -hex /tmp/c152.hex
  # then, per the addresses it prints:
  chrome --user-data-dir=$(mktemp -d) --headless=new https://127.0.0.1:<tcp>/
  chrome --user-data-dir=$(mktemp -d) --headless=new --enable-quic \
         --origin-to-force-quic-on=127.0.0.1:<udp> https://127.0.0.1:<udp>/
